<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Switzerland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Switzerland is a mountainous country in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> whose territory formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Accounts of the Holy Roman Empire list it among the lands, together with modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the Czech lands, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, that the empire encompassed at various times. The Swiss Confederation gradually became independent of imperial authority, a status formally recognised in 1648.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/switzerland.jpg" alt="Switzerland" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; Vector graphics: Marc Mongenet; Credits:; -xfi-; Zscout370; derivative work by Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Switzerland_(Pantone).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their speech is the ancestor of the Alemannic dialects of German, including Swabian, Alsatian and Swiss German, still spoken across south-western Germany, Alsace, <b>Switzerland</b>, Liechtenstein and Vorarlberg in Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Zürich founded a university in 1833, became the seat of the Federal Polytechnic in 1855 and grew into <b>Switzerland</b>’s largest city and financial centre, and the canton remains the most populous in Switzerland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Merchants and bankers traded through Genoa and across the Alpine passes to <b>Switzerland</b>, Germany and Flanders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On the French side, the loss of Geneva and the Vaud in 1536 shaped the Reformation in western <b>Switzerland</b>, and Savoy proper, French in language, eventually joined France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire was centred on German-speaking central Europe and at various times encompassed modern Germany, Austria, <b>Switzerland</b>, the Czech lands, northern Italy and the Low Countries, but it was never a unitary state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…grow within it: the County of Provence, the Dauphiné of Viennois, the Free County of Burgundy, the County of Savoy, which became a major Alpine state and later gave Italy its royal dynasty, and the cities and bishoprics of western <b>Switzerland</b>, whose lands later joined the Swiss Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
